Cactus Code Thorn TestSubcyclingCC
Author(s)    : Liwei Ji <jiliwei.phys@gmail.com>
Maintainer(s): Liwei Ji <jiliwei.phys@gmail.com>
Licence      : LGPL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

Regression test for subcycling-in-time with a nonlinear (limited)
prolongation operator on cell-centred data.

Under subcycling, CarpetX fills the refinement-boundary ghost cells of an
evolved grid function at every fine RK stage by evaluating the RK
dense-output polynomial on the coarse level (from the coarse start-of-step
state and stage derivatives) and then prolongating that single coarse
*state* in space, following AMReX's FillPatcher::fillRK order of
operations. For a linear prolongation operator this is identical to
prolongating each stage derivative and forming the polynomial on the fine
side, which is what the vertex-centred TestSubcyclingMC2 cases check
against their references. Only a nonlinear operator can tell the two
orderings apart, and CarpetX's limited operators ("eno", "minmod") are
nonlinear only in cell-centred directions.

This thorn therefore evolves the scalar wave equation on a cell-centred
{ccc} state with its own fourth-order centred Laplacian (five-point
stencil per direction, needs ghost_size = 3) and Kreiss-Oliger
dissipation, under ODESolvers RK4 with three refinement levels and
CarpetX::prolongation_type = "eno". The checked-in reference output is
generated by the state-based fill and locks in its behaviour with a
limited operator; the error against the analytic Gaussian solution must
stay comparable to the linear-operator TestSubcyclingMC2/test/gaussian
case, with no refinement-boundary artifacts.

2. Tests

  test/gaussian_eno.par   3 levels, RK4, use_subcycling = yes,
                          prolongation_type = "eno", prolongation_order = 3;
                          outputs state and error every 64 iterations
                          up to t = 2 (reference: test/gaussian_eno/)

The domain in gaussian_eno.par is offset from the pulse by a different
generic amount in each direction. On a grid that is mirror-symmetric
about the pulse, the ENO stencil selection compares candidate stencils
whose undivided differences tie exactly, and the tie is broken by
platform-dependent rounding, so the reference output would depend on the
compiler and CPU. Breaking the symmetry makes the stencil choice
deterministic across platforms.
